Of the $16$ subsets of $C_4$:

- already in $A$: $\{1,2\}, \{1,3\}, \{1,4\}$;
- subsets of an element of $A$ (cannot be added): $\{\}, \{1\}, \{2\}, \{3\}, \{4\}$;
- supersets of an element of $A$ (cannot be added): $\{1,2,3\}, \{1,2,4\}, \{1,3,4\}, \{1,2,3,4\}$;
- remaining candidates: $\{2,3\}, \{2,4\}, \{3,4\}, \{2,3,4\}$.

If $\{2,3,4\}$ is added, none of $\{2,3\}, \{2,4\}, \{3,4\}$ can be added (they are its subsets), giving the maximal family $\{\{1,2\},\{1,3\},\{1,4\},\{2,3,4\}\}$.

If any of the two-element candidates is added, $\{2,3,4\}$ is excluded, but the other two-element sets can all still be added, giving the maximal family $\{\{1,2\},\{1,3\},\{1,4\},\{2,3\},\{2,4\},\{3,4\}\}$.

**Answer: $\{\{1,2\},\{1,3\},\{1,4\},\{2,3,4\}\}$ and $\{\{1,2\},\{1,3\},\{1,4\},\{2,3\},\{2,4\},\{3,4\}\}$**
